<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · Dance · 1907 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Linstead Market</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Jamaican folk song about a mother who takes her ackee fruit to Linstead market but sells none, so her children will go hungry.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jamaicans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English (Jamaican)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Jamaica</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first printed 1907</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament · Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional Jamaican; first printed in Walter Jekyll's Jamaican Song and Story (1907))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linstead-market">Linstead Market</a>&quot; is a Jamaican folk song of the mento type. It tells of a mother who takes her ackee fruit to the market but sells none of it, so that her children will go hungry. Possibly its earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> with words is in Walter Jekyll's book Jamaican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Story (1907), where it is item 121, on pages 219–220; Jekyll wrote its melody in 6/8 time, while every later printing named here is in 2/4 or 4/4.</p><p>In 1925 Helen H. Roberts published a study of folk-song variants based on her fieldwork in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. She reproduced Jekyll's version and twelve variants, among them one sung in Christiana; in some of them &quot;Sollas market&quot; takes the place of &quot;Linstead market&quot; (Sollas market became Jubilee Market, on West Queen Street in Kingston). In 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> University Press published the song in Olive Lewin's collection of Jamaican folk songs for children. Lewin wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linstead-market">Linstead Market</a> was still a picturesque small-town market, and that the song, now often taken much faster for dancing, was first sung slowly and plaintively by a mother who could not sell enough at the market to feed her children. The quatty it names was worth a penny and a half; it was not a minted coin but a standard unit of buying and selling, goods being sold in amounts worth a quatty or multiples of it.</p><p>The song has been arranged many times. Arthur Benjamin published it for solo voice and piano (Boosey &amp; Hawkes, 1947), and A. H. Green for choir (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> University Press, 1967). The King's Singers recorded it in 1992, and the English folk group The Spinners recorded it with their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> singer Cliff Hall; the Cuban group La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a> Matancera covered it in 1957 in English and Spanish, and the pianist Monty Alexander released a live version, recorded in 2012, on an album of 2014, calling it &quot;one of the staples of the Mento repertoire&quot;. Brian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> Nabors arranged it for string quartet in 2018. It also became a hymn tune: as LINSTEAD, arranged by Doreen Potter in Break Not the Circle (Hope Publishing, 1975) with a text by Fred Kaan, it appears in several hymnals, among them the Presbyterian Hymnal (1990) and Hymns Ancient &amp; Modern New Standard (1983).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A market woman's lament. She has carried her ackee to Linstead market and sold not even a quatty's worth, and goes home to a night with no light and nothing to eat, for herself or her children. Sung slowly it is plaintive; taken fast, as it often is today, it became a dance tune.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Cyar mi ackee go a Linstead market,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Not a quatty wut sell. Oh what a losses!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Not a quatty worth sell. Me carry me akee a</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Linstead market. Not a quatty worth sell. Oh not a</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>light, not a bite! Not a quatty worth sell.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Walter Jekyll, Jamaican Song and Story (1907), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linstead_Market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
